insert into public.verse_commentary (ref, note) values
  ('2CO.3.14', 'Paul is comparing two ways of reading Scripture. Some read Moses and still see it as the final word. Paul says reading Moses without Christ is like reading a letter with the envelope still sealed — the full message is hidden until you find the key. Christ is the key.'),
  ('2CO.3.15', 'Every time Moses is read, a veil covers the heart, Paul says. He''s not criticising Jewish people. He''s making a point about revelation: the Old Covenant was always pointing forward to something it couldn''t fully show. Like a road sign is not the destination.'),
  ('2CO.3.16', 'The word ''turn'' here is the same Greek word for repentance — turning back. The moment you turn toward God, the veil comes off. Not after you''ve cleaned up. Not after you''ve been good enough. The moment you simply turn — it is removed.'),
  ('2CO.3.17', 'This is one of the most astonishing sentences in the Bible. Paul says the Lord IS the Spirit. Not ''like'' the Spirit. Not ''represented by'' the Spirit. IS. The same God who parted the Red Sea, who spoke from the burning bush — you encounter Him right now through the Holy Spirit. And where He is, freedom is.'),
  ('2CO.3.18', 'This is the most hopeful sentence in this passage. Being transformed ''from glory to glory'' means it''s a process, not a destination. You are not expected to arrive all at once. Every time you turn to God, something changes in you. A little more. A little more. Like a face turning toward the sun.'),
  ('GEN.1.1', 'Six words in Hebrew. No argument, no defence, no attempt to prove anything — just a statement made calmly. Everything else in the Bible rests on this sentence being true, and it is offered without a single supporting clause.'),
  ('GEN.1.3', 'Notice the mechanism: God speaks, and it is. Not builds, not fights, not shapes from something pre-existing. In the creation stories of Israel’s neighbours, making a world took a war. Here it takes a sentence.'),
  ('GEN.1.27', 'In the ancient Near East, "the image of God" was a phrase used of kings, and of statues placed in temples to represent an absent deity. Genesis applies it to every human being without exception. That was, in its context, a genuinely radical redistribution.'),
  ('PSA.23.1', 'David had actually done this job. He knew what it cost to keep sheep alive — the early mornings, the predators, the endless counting. When he says the Lord is his shepherd, he is not picking a gentle image. He is naming someone who works hard on his behalf.'),
  ('PSA.23.4', 'The psalm switches from talking about God to talking to God exactly here, in the valley. That is not a literary accident. Difficulty is often where a relationship stops being theoretical.'),
  ('PSA.23.6', 'The word behind "follow" is stronger than it sounds in English — it is closer to "pursue", the word used for chasing down an enemy. Goodness and mercy are not trailing politely behind you. They are hunting you down.'),
  ('JHN.1.1', 'Three claims in one sentence, each one raising the stakes: the Word already existed, the Word was with God, the Word was God. John spends the rest of the Gospel showing you the face of the person he has just described.'),
  ('JHN.1.5', 'The verb translated "overcome" can also mean "grasp" or "understand" — and John almost certainly intends both. The darkness neither defeated the light nor comprehended it.'),
  ('JHN.1.14', 'This is the sentence Christianity stands or falls on. Not that God sent a message, or a prophet, or a principle — that God took on a body, with all the hunger and tiredness and mortality that implies, and lived here.')
